- So I've realized that
gosh, a lot of these artists

00:08:16.458 --> 00:08:17.625
actually aren't that happy,

00:08:17.625 --> 00:08:18.708
even though they
have these philosophies,

00:08:18.708 --> 00:08:19.625
they don't have any income.

00:08:19.625 --> 00:08:20.917
They don't have any,--
- Sure.

00:08:20.917 --> 00:08:23.750
- money. They don't know how to
build traction or build a brand,

00:08:23.750 --> 00:08:24.583
and so,--
- Well, they're probably

00:08:24.583 --> 00:08:26.917
like India, they get
happiness out of complaining

00:08:26.917 --> 00:08:28.250
and being starving
(Erik laughs)

00:08:28.250 --> 00:08:29.041
and the negativity.

00:08:29.041 --> 00:08:30.500
By the way, I'm that way too.

00:08:30.500 --> 00:08:32.417
It's actually why, you
know, you want to hear this?

00:08:32.417 --> 00:08:34.125
It's actually why I
really associate with that.

00:08:34.125 --> 00:08:36.041
I like the pain of the process.

00:08:36.041 --> 00:08:36.875
- Mhmmm.

00:08:36.875 --> 00:08:39.041
- I really do, like, the
reason I keep fantasizing

00:08:39.041 --> 00:08:41.125
about melting is I'm excited.

00:08:41.125 --> 00:08:43.291
By the way, you think I'm
excited to fire 280 people

00:08:43.291 --> 00:08:45.792
because a third of
our clients fire us?

00:08:45.792 --> 00:08:47.166
You think that's
interesting to me?

00:08:47.166 --> 00:08:48.583
You don't think I think
about that every day

00:08:48.583 --> 00:08:50.375
in a world where we've
had nothing but growth?

00:08:50.375 --> 00:08:53.625
I'm scared shitless of the
pain that's going to bring me.

00:08:53.625 --> 00:08:56.917
Truth is, you have no options.

00:08:56.917 --> 00:09:00.000
- The way things go.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:03.291
If you're an artist
looking to build a brand,

00:09:03.291 --> 00:09:05.166
I think there is a lot to learn.

00:09:05.166 --> 00:09:06.291
That's what I
found is that artists

00:09:06.291 --> 00:09:09.375
did not have
that grind of learning

00:09:09.375 --> 00:09:11.500
how to amplify
their brand to scale.

00:09:11.500 --> 00:09:13.542
How to push
their genius out there,

00:09:13.542 --> 00:09:14.625
and so that's where I come--

00:09:14.625 --> 00:09:18.417
- So you were business-y,
went to art, and then

00:09:18.417 --> 00:09:20.417
went back to 
business-y around art.

00:09:20.417 --> 00:09:21.500
- Right.
- I love that.

00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:23.166
- And this time is
when I started to create.

00:09:23.166 --> 00:09:25.291
I'd never painted before, but
it was so fascinating to me.

00:09:25.291 --> 00:09:27.166
- You're so good at it!

00:09:27.166 --> 00:09:28.583
No really, he is.

00:09:28.583 --> 00:09:29.708
- [DRock] He's very intelligent.

00:09:29.708 --> 00:09:31.166
- Are you fascinated by that?

00:09:31.166 --> 00:09:32.792
One thing I tell
a lot of people is,

00:09:32.792 --> 00:09:35.625
people ask me, what's
my passion Gary, right?

00:09:35.625 --> 00:09:37.417
And I'm like, well if
you never had a hot dog,

00:09:37.417 --> 00:09:39.708
would you know if
you liked eating hot dogs?

00:09:39.708 --> 00:09:41.959
Did you find it,
were you confused

00:09:41.959 --> 00:09:43.750
that you were good at it?

00:09:43.750 --> 00:09:45.041
- Good is a relative term.

00:09:45.041 --> 00:09:46.500
I sucked when I started,

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:47.834
but I loved the process.

00:09:47.834 --> 00:09:49.583
I loved the idea of creating,

00:09:49.583 --> 00:09:51.792
I loved the idea
of getting better.

00:09:51.792 --> 00:09:53.208
Painting was
just where it kind of

00:09:53.208 --> 00:09:54.375
opened up and I got branded

00:09:54.375 --> 00:09:55.750
as a graffiti artist early on,

00:09:55.750 --> 00:09:58.417
partly because
I loved it so much,

00:09:58.417 --> 00:09:59.417
I loved their philosophy,

00:09:59.417 --> 00:10:00.959
I loved street artists,

00:10:00.959 --> 00:10:02.083
and also I started doing it

00:10:02.083 --> 00:10:02.917
and hanging out with them.

00:10:02.917 --> 00:10:05.250
But I also started
landscape painting,

00:10:05.250 --> 00:10:07.000
figure drawing, writing poetry,

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:08.458
doing photography,

00:10:08.458 --> 00:10:11.667
so that was the
gateway drug to understanding

00:10:11.667 --> 00:10:13.041
creativity as a whole.

00:10:13.041 --> 00:10:15.917
And creativity is not
just painting or graffiti,

00:10:15.917 --> 00:10:17.333
it's a way of life,
it's an energy.

00:10:17.333 --> 00:10:18.792
- It's a mindset.
- It's a mindset.

00:10:18.792 --> 00:10:21.875
So that's why I took
it not only into writing

00:10:21.875 --> 00:10:25.291
and into sculpture
but also into business,

00:10:25.291 --> 00:10:27.667
because business is
all about being creative.

00:10:27.667 --> 00:10:28.542
- Of course.

00:10:28.542 --> 00:10:29.917
- Marketing, strategy...

00:10:29.917 --> 00:10:32.917
- So actually now I'm really
deep into the vortex of this.

00:10:32.917 --> 00:10:34.583
So you start making shit, right?

00:10:34.583 --> 00:10:36.583
You've got three
kids at home and the wife.

00:10:36.583 --> 00:10:38.708
What happens?

00:10:38.708 --> 00:10:42.667
Give me a quick five second
2000 to 2005, like,

00:10:42.667 --> 00:10:43.708
what happened?

00:10:43.708 --> 00:10:46.875
- I started writing all
these ideas that artists

00:10:46.875 --> 00:10:49.750
had the genius but no
discipline to how to get

00:10:49.750 --> 00:10:51.375
their message to scale.

00:10:51.375 --> 00:10:53.583
Businesses had this
incredible discipline

00:10:53.583 --> 00:10:57.583
but no disruptive
thinking or curiosity,

00:10:57.583 --> 00:11:00.542
so that's where I
wrote a presentation about

00:11:00.542 --> 00:11:01.875
how to merge the two.

00:11:01.875 --> 00:11:02.542
I called it The Art of Vision,

00:11:02.542 --> 00:11:04.000
started painting onstage,

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:05.208
started cranking rock music.

00:11:05.208 --> 00:11:07.166
- How did you
get your first gig?

00:11:07.166 --> 00:11:09.083
- Marketing. Hustling.

00:11:09.083 --> 00:11:13.708
My wife and I created
a manufactured brochure.

00:11:13.708 --> 00:11:17.250
We taped a presentation,
so we asked a local college,

00:11:17.250 --> 00:11:19.291
hey can we come and
deliver a presentation for free.

00:11:19.291 --> 00:11:20.875
- How much did they pay you?

00:11:20.875 --> 00:11:22.417
- We got paid zero.

00:11:22.417 --> 00:11:25.834
And we also paid a film
crew to come and film us.

00:11:25.834 --> 00:11:26.667
- Negative!

00:11:26.667 --> 00:11:28.875
- And make it look like,
make it look like this is

00:11:28.875 --> 00:11:29.750
something that it isn't.

00:11:29.750 --> 00:11:31.792
- How many presentations
did you give for free

00:11:31.792 --> 00:11:33.875
in the early days?

00:11:33.875 --> 00:11:35.875
- Fortunately, and I know
where you're going with this...

00:11:35.875 --> 00:11:37.125
- But that's
okay, I want your truth,

00:11:37.125 --> 00:11:39.792
I don't want you to
appease my strategy.

00:11:39.792 --> 00:11:42.000
- Yeah, I did probably three.

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:44.291
- No, no you have to understand.

00:11:44.291 --> 00:11:46.417
Enormous amounts of
people feel like that's

00:11:46.417 --> 00:11:48.125
three too many.

00:11:48.125 --> 00:11:51.208
- If you want to be
a professional speaker,

00:11:51.208 --> 00:11:53.125
you should be doing
hundreds of present--

00:11:53.125 --> 00:11:54.917
- If you want to be
a professional anything

00:11:54.917 --> 00:11:56.542
that nobody else
wants to pay you for,

00:11:56.542 --> 00:11:58.875
you should do it for free.

00:11:58.875 --> 00:12:00.083
That's your
only way of doing it.

00:12:00.083 --> 00:12:02.125
- When I first spoke, I sucked!

00:12:02.125 --> 00:12:03.875
My wife was super...

00:12:03.875 --> 00:12:05.250
- Disappointed in you?

00:12:05.250 --> 00:12:06.625
- She was super supportive.

00:12:06.625 --> 00:12:07.458
(woman laughs)

00:12:07.458 --> 00:12:08.959
- She's like "You suck"!

00:12:08.959 --> 00:12:11.291
- Somehow she
was able to muster,

00:12:11.291 --> 00:12:12.959
instead of saying "You
suck", she still was able

00:12:12.959 --> 00:12:15.417
to say, "Let's go Hon".

00:12:15.417 --> 00:12:17.834
So I felt
affirmed, I'm like okay--

00:12:17.834 --> 00:12:19.708
- But she's more
glass half full, right?

00:12:19.708 --> 00:12:21.291
And you're more
glass half empty.

00:12:21.291 --> 00:12:22.208
- I would say so.

00:12:22.208 --> 00:12:24.792
- I know you guys, not
that you're so horrible,

00:12:24.792 --> 00:12:27.458
but I mean she is
a little bit more,

00:12:27.458 --> 00:12:29.625
like her natural
reaction is a little bit

00:12:29.625 --> 00:12:30.583
more positive.

00:12:30.583 --> 00:12:31.667
- She's my Yoko Ono.

00:12:31.667 --> 00:12:34.250
So I'm this kind of
confused John Lennon.

00:12:34.250 --> 00:12:36.125
I've got ideas but
insecure about putting them

00:12:36.125 --> 00:12:37.625
out there.
That's the truth.

00:12:37.625 --> 00:12:39.583
She's always been
behind me and pushing

00:12:39.583 --> 00:12:41.041
that out there,
like "You know what?

00:12:41.041 --> 00:12:42.083
"I think this is
gonna be awesome.

00:12:42.083 --> 00:12:43.208
"You know what?
They're gonna go for this".

00:12:43.208 --> 00:12:45.875
And so I had a lot of
support and encouragement

00:12:45.875 --> 00:12:48.333
early on from her,
and I ended up doing

00:12:48.333 --> 00:12:49.750
a presentation.

00:12:49.750 --> 00:12:52.583
She hustled for
me to speak to some

00:12:52.583 --> 00:12:54.083
American healthcare association,

00:12:54.083 --> 00:12:55.583
kind of a national meeting,

00:12:55.583 --> 00:12:58.041
and inside there's 50
state associations, so--

00:12:58.041 --> 00:12:59.250
- And that blew it up!

00:12:59.250 --> 00:13:00.375
- It's kind of
like hitting the lotto.

00:13:00.375 --> 00:13:01.208
- That was the moment.

00:13:01.208 --> 00:13:02.041
- [Woman] That was the moment,

00:13:02.041 --> 00:13:03.208
and you just kind
of learn as you go.

00:13:03.208 --> 00:13:03.959
- Yeah a hundred percent.

00:13:03.959 --> 00:13:05.458
- [Woman] What's the
best way is you start

00:13:05.458 --> 00:13:08.291
big and you go
backwards, you reverse engineer.

00:13:08.291 --> 00:13:09.083
- Hundred percent.

00:13:09.083 --> 00:13:10.291
We're about to
get into a phone call.

00:13:10.291 --> 00:13:12.417
I want to do two things
before we get there, Andy.

00:13:12.417 --> 00:13:14.333
Oh you're Facebook, Instagram,
start putting your phone...

00:13:14.333 --> 00:13:15.500
Oh you're doing Facebook!

00:13:15.500 --> 00:13:16.333
Instagram, sorry.

00:13:16.333 --> 00:13:19.083
Facebook Live is doing
the phone numbers this time.

00:13:19.083 --> 00:13:21.208
Please post your phone numbers

00:13:21.208 --> 00:13:23.208
and we will call somebody.

00:13:23.208 --> 00:13:24.458
- I wanna make one
connection really quick.

00:13:24.458 --> 00:13:26.417
- You're gonna be
able to do that for sure.

00:13:26.417 --> 00:13:27.667
I got two other
things before we do calls.

00:13:27.667 --> 00:13:28.667
Go ahead, you start.

00:13:28.667 --> 00:13:30.083
- Okay. You gave a
presentation in Dublin

00:13:30.083 --> 00:13:31.041
called Clouds and Dirt?

00:13:31.041 --> 00:13:31.834
- Yes.

00:13:31.834 --> 00:13:33.792
- That is the
spark and the grind.

00:13:33.792 --> 00:13:35.917
It is the idea and execution.

00:13:35.917 --> 00:13:37.875
- Wait you just wrote
the Clouds and Dirt book

00:13:37.875 --> 00:13:39.208
that I've been
wanting to write one day?

00:13:39.208 --> 00:13:40.208
I'm very upset right now!

00:13:40.208 --> 00:13:42.166
- I beat you to market.

00:13:42.166 --> 00:13:42.959
- Is that the spiel?

00:13:42.959 --> 00:13:43.959
- That is exactly
it, and that's why

00:13:43.959 --> 00:13:45.542
I brought Rodin's Thinker,
because he's a man

00:13:45.542 --> 00:13:48.583
of great muscularity,
so it's not just this sage,

00:13:48.583 --> 00:13:50.542
he's a doer, and so he's
kind of been my little mascot

00:13:50.542 --> 00:13:52.041
that I've been
bringing around with me.

00:13:52.041 --> 00:13:54.208
- This one.
- Yes, the Heisman trophy.

00:13:54.208 --> 00:13:59.041
- And so this, why don't
we study for another second

00:13:59.041 --> 00:14:00.375
the book.

00:14:00.375 --> 00:14:02.125
You've written several books?

00:14:02.125 --> 00:14:04.125
- This is my second
major published release.

00:14:04.125 --> 00:14:05.875
- And did some direct
to consumer stuff before,

00:14:05.875 --> 00:14:07.708
but you feel like
this is your second book.

00:14:07.708 --> 00:14:08.417
- This is my second baby.

00:14:08.417 --> 00:14:10.542
- How do you feel
that process has been?

00:14:10.542 --> 00:14:11.750
This is your second book.

00:14:11.750 --> 00:14:12.917
How do you
compare it to your first?

00:14:12.917 --> 00:14:14.166
What do you
think you did better?

00:14:14.166 --> 00:14:15.625
What's different, da da da...

00:14:15.625 --> 00:14:17.250
- The writing
process was similar

00:14:17.250 --> 00:14:19.166
in that I love it.
I love the grind of writing.

00:14:19.166 --> 00:14:20.834
- You love writing?
- I love writing.

00:14:20.834 --> 00:14:21.959
- I hate writing.

00:14:21.959 --> 00:14:22.875
I don't write.

00:14:22.875 --> 00:14:25.750
- What I don't
like is the marketing.

00:14:25.750 --> 00:14:27.500
The fact that I've gotta like,

00:14:27.500 --> 00:14:29.125
Hey everyone...

00:14:29.125 --> 00:14:32.583
I actually don't care--
- You hate selling?

00:14:32.583 --> 00:14:35.333
- I know you love selling--

00:14:35.333 --> 00:14:36.166
- No, no, be careful.

00:14:36.166 --> 00:14:37.959
I've said this, some of
you that really know me,

00:14:37.959 --> 00:14:40.208
I actually hate microselling.

00:14:40.208 --> 00:14:42.917
The way I like selling
is giving so much value,

00:14:42.917 --> 00:14:44.250
and building so much brand,

00:14:44.250 --> 00:14:45.834
that it's sold before,

00:14:45.834 --> 00:14:46.834
I hate selling.

00:14:46.834 --> 00:14:50.959
I actually decided not
to raise a fund last year.

00:14:50.959 --> 00:14:53.750
I was raising a big fund,
Vayner Capital, a new fund.

00:14:53.750 --> 00:14:56.959
And I stopped 'cause
I so disliked asking

00:14:56.959 --> 00:14:58.166
people for money.
- I hear you.

00:14:58.166 --> 00:14:59.708
- I hate it. I hate selling.

00:14:59.708 --> 00:15:00.458
I hate it.

00:15:00.458 --> 00:15:02.792
- I hate the fact that
this is tipped up like this

00:15:02.792 --> 00:15:04.542
like we're trying to pimp it.

00:15:04.542 --> 00:15:05.959
- I agree. No, no--
- I would prefer--

00:15:05.959 --> 00:15:07.875
- Like, yeah.
- Let's put those over there.

00:15:07.875 --> 00:15:09.917
Let's just talk--
- I agree.

00:15:09.917 --> 00:15:11.375
- We'll let the
thinker stand in--

00:15:11.375 --> 00:15:12.375
- I agree. I get it.

00:15:12.375 --> 00:15:13.917
I mean, every time
when I'm on book tour

00:15:13.917 --> 00:15:14.959
and I'm doing
something like this,

00:15:14.959 --> 00:15:16.417
they're like,
"Why don't you tell us

00:15:16.417 --> 00:15:17.458
"why they should buy the book?"

00:15:17.458 --> 00:15:18.792
I'm like, "They shouldn't!"
- Yeah.

00:15:18.792 --> 00:15:20.875
(group laughter)
- Don't buy it!

00:15:20.875 --> 00:15:22.667
- I'm not interested in going
through chapter by chapter

00:15:22.667 --> 00:15:23.708
or anything about that book.

00:15:23.708 --> 00:15:24.750
It's the brand.

00:15:24.750 --> 00:15:26.792
That's one channel of my brand.

00:15:26.792 --> 00:15:28.500
- By the way, I'm thinking
about calling it "Crushing It."

00:15:28.500 --> 00:15:29.375
Not "Crushed It."

00:15:29.375 --> 00:15:30.834
I'm writing my next
one called "Crushing It."

00:15:30.834 --> 00:15:31.792
- Okay.
- It's a follow up.

00:15:31.792 --> 00:15:33.917
And the process, but I
do it totally different.

00:15:33.917 --> 00:15:34.750
I have a ghost writer.

00:15:34.750 --> 00:15:36.041
I'm recording it,
like doing interviews,

00:15:36.041 --> 00:15:40.375
sliding down icy driveways
in Colorado while I do it,

00:15:40.375 --> 00:15:42.959
but I was curious how
everybody's different process.

00:15:42.959 --> 00:15:44.792
I'm always curious
about the second time.

00:15:44.792 --> 00:15:46.125
- So, mine is a verbal vomit.

00:15:46.125 --> 00:15:47.708
I write every single day.

00:15:47.708 --> 00:15:49.291
That's what I do.
That's how I process thought.

00:15:49.291 --> 00:15:50.291
That's how I think.

00:15:50.291 --> 00:15:52.000
So, I write, write, write.

00:15:52.000 --> 00:15:53.041
It's called a verbal vomit.

00:15:53.041 --> 00:15:54.583
And then I send it to editors.

00:15:54.583 --> 00:15:55.667
(Gary murmurs) And
like, Random House.

00:15:55.667 --> 00:15:56.875
- Yeah, yeah.
- And they're incredible,

00:15:56.875 --> 00:15:58.333
because they put it in
bite-sized chunks

00:15:58.333 --> 00:16:00.000
where consumers are like,
"Oh, yeah. That makes sense.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:00.708
"Oh yeah, That makes sense."

00:16:00.708 --> 00:16:03.083
But when I think,
I'm just writing.

00:16:03.083 --> 00:16:03.625
So that's what it is.

00:16:03.625 --> 00:16:06.417
- You have an ungodly
amount of 20 to 25-year-old

00:16:06.417 --> 00:16:09.500
entrepreneurs,
wannabe entrepreneurs,

00:16:09.500 --> 00:16:10.959
and we have the full gamut,

00:16:10.959 --> 00:16:14.208
but growth on
Instagram for me has translated

00:16:14.208 --> 00:16:16.083
to a much younger audience.

00:16:16.083 --> 00:16:18.375
What do you want to tell
those characters right now?

00:16:18.375 --> 00:16:20.166
Let's do our first phone call.

00:16:20.166 --> 00:16:22.959
What's on your
mind for that demo?

00:16:22.959 --> 00:16:24.125
I know this is generalizing.

00:16:24.125 --> 00:16:26.375
- That structure
creates freedom.

00:16:26.375 --> 00:16:29.166
So, that's kind of
the paradox of creativity

00:16:29.166 --> 00:16:30.000
or innovation.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:32.458
That discipline and
routine and hard work

00:16:32.458 --> 00:16:35.125
is what makes way
for constant creativity.

00:16:35.125 --> 00:16:36.625
And then, of course, patience.

00:16:36.625 --> 00:16:38.208
- You agree with that?

00:16:38.208 --> 00:16:40.083
- I'm living that.

00:16:40.083 --> 00:16:41.500
It's more than just agreeing.

00:16:41.500 --> 00:16:42.834
- It's not something
that came natural to you

00:16:42.834 --> 00:16:43.875
in your younger age?

00:16:43.875 --> 00:16:44.708
